## =============================================================================
## Sirius — environment template
## =============================================================================
## Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values:
##
##     cp .env.example .env
##
## `.env` is git-ignored. `.env.example` is committed as the reference.
## -----------------------------------------------------------------------------


## --- Database ----------------------------------------------------------------
## PostgreSQL connection string. Read by `prisma.config.ts` (via dotenv) for
## CLI commands, and by `lib/prisma.ts` at runtime.
##
## THIS MUST NEVER BE PRODUCTION.
##
## A checkout points at a local Postgres or at its own Neon dev branch — never
## at the production endpoint, not "just to read", not "just this once". Seeds,
## fixtures and `prisma db push` are the obvious hazards, but they are not the
## main one: simply using the app writes rows. Opening the simulator starts an
## attempt. That is what the page is for, and no amount of care while typing
## commands prevents it.
##
## On Neon, branch off production and use the branch's own endpoint — it gets a
## distinct `ep-...` hostname, so which database you are on is a fact you can
## read rather than a thing you remember. Throw the branch away and re-cut it
## whenever it drifts:
##
##   neon branches create --name dev/<your-name> --parent main
##   neon branches reset dev/<your-name> --parent
##
## Every process prints the endpoint it is about to use on startup — that line
## is the check. If it names production, stop. See `DATABASE.md`.
##
## Local Postgres:
##   DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/sirius?schema=public"
##
## No Postgres installed? Either of these gives you one in seconds:
##   npx prisma dev      # local Postgres, runs in your terminal
##   npx create-db       # free hosted Prisma Postgres
##
## Then apply the schema:  npx prisma db push
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/sirius?schema=public"


## How many Postgres connections the pool may open. Defaults to 1.
##
## Leave it at 1 for `npx prisma dev`: that server is PGlite (Postgres compiled
## to WebAssembly in a single process) and its TCP shim cannot serve parallel
## connections — doing so crosses the wire protocol and Postgres reports
## `08P01: bind message supplies N parameters, but prepared statement ""
## requires 0`. One connection makes pg queue queries instead, which it handles
## perfectly.
##
## On a real Postgres (Neon, Supabase, RDS, a local postgres service) set 10.
## Behind PgBouncer in transaction mode, keep it low and add ?pgbouncer=true to
## DATABASE_URL.
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX="10"


## --- Authentication (Auth.js / NextAuth v5) ----------------------------------
## Google is the only sign-in provider.
##
## 1. AUTH_SECRET signs the session cookie. Generate one with:
##      npx auth secret          (or: openssl rand -base64 32)
##    A different secret invalidates every existing session, so keep it stable.
##
## 2. Create an OAuth client at https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
##    → "Create credentials" → "OAuth client ID" → "Web application".
##    Authorised redirect URIs must include, exactly:
##      http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google      (development)
##      https://your-domain.com/api/auth/callback/google    (production)
##
## Leaving the Google keys blank is safe: the sign-in page detects it and shows
## setup instructions instead of a broken button.
AUTH_SECRET=""
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=""
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=""

## Only needed if the deployed URL cannot be inferred (behind a proxy, or a
## custom domain that differs from the host header).
# AUTH_URL="https://your-domain.com"


## --- University data (College Scorecard) -------------------------------------
## The US Department of Education's open dataset supplies admission rates, SAT
## midpoints and tuition for the seed. Get a free key in about a minute at
## https://api.data.gov/signup/
##
## A real key is REQUIRED to seed the full list. The seed pages through the API
## three times (100 rows a page), and the shared DEMO_KEY allows only about
## thirty requests an hour per IP — enough to try one page, not to fill the
## table. Without a key the seed logs the rate-limit error and leaves the
## existing rows untouched rather than half-importing.
SCORECARD_API_KEY=""


## --- Test content import -----------------------------------------------------
## Shared secret for `POST /api/tests/import`, sent as `Authorization: Bearer …`.
## The endpoint refuses every request in production unless this is set, so your
## question bank cannot be overwritten by an anonymous caller.
##
## Generate one:  node -e "console.log(crypto.randomUUID())"
TEST_IMPORT_TOKEN=""
